# Golden

## Trustworks Essentials framework knowledge

<!-- essentials-init: essentials 0.60.0 -->

For ANY question about Trustworks Essentials APIs, modules, or patterns, **always consult the `essentials-docs` skill** from the `essentials` plugin. It auto-loads on Essentials-related questions and on code signals (imports of `dk.trustworks.essentials.*`, type names like `StatefulAggregate`, `DurableQueues`, `EventStore`, `FencedLock`, `Inbox`, `Outbox`, `UnitOfWork`, `Decider`, `EventProcessor`).

Do **not** search the web or guess — Essentials has its own opinionated patterns documented under the plugin's `references/llm/`. Before editing Essentials code, the skill consults the plugin's design guide and the traps index automatically. Trust its advisory output.

If the `essentials` plugin is not installed in this Claude Code session, add its marketplace with `/plugin marketplace add trustworksdk/essentials-project`, then run `/plugin install essentials@essentials-marketplace`.

The comment above records the Essentials version this project was scaffolded against. Run **`/essentials:upgrade`** to bring the project up to what the installed plugin ships — a refreshed slice-rules pointer, the slice-manifest lint gate, and a conformance check against the current stack contract (S1–S11). It reports before it writes, and it never touches source or version pins.

## Project pins (do not invent — these are fixed by `/essentials:init`)

- **Package root**: `com.example.golden` (every `package` declaration and the Maven coordinates derive from this)
- **Maven groupId / artifactId**: `com.example` / `golden` (the reactor parent is `golden-parent`)
- **Stack version pins** (Spring Boot, Java language level, Trustworks Essentials, every other dependency): authoritative in the root `pom.xml`'s `<properties>` and `frontend/package.json`. Read them before any planning step that names a dependency or version. Do **not** propose your own pins, do **not** "upgrade to latest", do **not** invent versions that look plausible. Upgrades require an ADR — they are not implementation choices.

## Code locations for new code

When adding new features (whether by hand or via any implementer tooling), use these paths. Do **not** create new source files at the project root.

- Backend (production): `backend/src/main/java/com/example/golden/...`
- Backend (tests): `backend/src/test/java/com/example/golden/...`
- Backend resources / config: `backend/src/main/resources/`
- Frontend (production): `frontend/src/...`
- Frontend (tests): co-located with the source they test (`*.test.ts` / `*.test.tsx`), run by vitest
- Generated API client (do **not** hand-edit, do **not** commit): `frontend/src/shared/api/generated/` and `frontend/src/shared/api/model/`
- OpenAPI contract (generated by `mvn verify`, committed, never hand-edited): `contracts/openapi.json`

A planner's design spec that says "Greenfield. All paths are new" while omitting the `backend/` / `frontend/` prefix is a contradiction with this scaffold and must be corrected, not followed literally.

## Stack

- **Backend**: Java, Spring Boot with WebMvc (servlet), Maven, Trustworks Essentials
  - Persistence: MongoDB (`spring-boot-starter-mongodb`), run as a replica set: Essentials' Mongo transactions need one
- **Frontend**: React, TypeScript, Vite, Tailwind CSS, TanStack Query, Orval (generated API client), built into the backend JAR (embedded: one origin, no CORS, no API base URL)
- **API contract**: SpringDoc → `contracts/openapi.json` → Orval → TypeScript fetch client

## Project layout

```
pom.xml                  # Reactor parent: every version property and BOM import
backend/pom.xml          # The application module
backend/compose.yml      # Development MongoDB, started by spring-boot:run
backend/src/main/java/com/example/golden/
  Application.java
  config/                # Application-wide Spring wiring (web configurer, security, SPA filter)
  <bc>/                  # one per bounded context — none is scaffolded; /essentials:add-slice creates them
backend/src/main/resources/application.yml
backend/src/test/java/com/example/golden/
                         # IntegrationTestBase: one MongoDB container per test JVM; extend it for every integration test
                         # ApplicationContextIT: the whole context starts and reports healthy
                         # OpenApiContractIT: regenerates contracts/openapi.json (S7)
contracts/openapi.json   # The API contract, generated by `mvn verify` and committed
frontend/                # React SPA (built into the backend JAR)
  src/app/               #   app shell and routing
  src/domains/<bc>/      #   per-context components/hooks/pages (one component per use-case)
  src/shared/api/        #   custom-fetch.ts (yours) + the generated client (not committed)
dev.sh                   # Dev convenience script
```

## Commands

Run Maven from the project root. `./mvnw` is the Maven wrapper; when the project has none, use `mvn` (`./dev.sh` falls back on its own).

### Build and test

```bash
./mvnw package                    # compile, unit tests, the JAR with the SPA built into it
./mvnw package -Pskip-frontend    # the same without Node/npm, for backend-only work
./mvnw verify -Pskip-frontend     # + integration tests (Docker) + regenerates contracts/openapi.json
./mvnw -pl backend test -Dtest=ClassName             # one unit test class
./mvnw -pl backend test -Dtest="ClassName#method"    # one unit test method
```

Integration tests (`*IT`, `*IntegrationTest`) run under Failsafe on `verify` and need Docker: each test JVM starts one MongoDB container through `IntegrationTestBase`.

### Inner-loop integration tests (RED → GREEN iteration)

While iterating to make a failing test go green, run **only the changed IT class**, compiling first so the test does not run stale bytecode:

```bash
./mvnw -pl backend test-compile failsafe:integration-test failsafe:verify -Dit.test=ChangedIT
./mvnw -pl backend test-compile failsafe:integration-test failsafe:verify -Dit.test='ChangedIT#method_name'
```

Run full `./mvnw verify` once at the end of GREEN, before the save-point commit.

`IntegrationTestBase` already follows the fast-suite recipe: one container per JVM, and a small Spring context cache that makes a second context visible. What keeps that recipe safe with Essentials — truncate read-model tables only, **never an `*_events` table with `RESTART IDENTITY`**, and rebuild a read model through `onSubscriptionsReset` plus `resetAllSubscriptions()` — is in the `essentials-docs` skill (`LLM-foundation-test.md` § Fast integration tests). Container reuse across builds is opt-in there.

### Development

```bash
./dev.sh              # backend + frontend together
./dev.sh backend      # spring-boot:run on :8080 (starts MongoDB via Docker Compose)
./dev.sh frontend     # vite dev server on :5173 (proxies /api/* to :8080)
./dev.sh generate     # regenerate contracts/openapi.json (OpenApiContractIT, needs Docker) + the client
```

The database is managed by Spring Boot's Docker Compose support: `spring-boot:run` starts `backend/compose.yml` and stops it on shutdown. Tests never use it; they start their own container.

### Frontend

```bash
cd frontend
npm install               # first time; creates package-lock.json, which the build's `npm ci` needs (commit it)
npm run dev               # vite dev server with HMR
npm run build             # tsc + vite build
npm test                  # vitest (watch mode)
npm test -- --run         # vitest single run
npm run lint              # eslint
npm run generate-api      # orval (regenerate from contracts/openapi.json)
```

## API contract workflow

`contracts/openapi.json` is **generated from the code, never hand-written, and committed**. SpringDoc serves the spec at `http://localhost:8080/v3/api-docs` from the `@RestController` endpoints and their DTOs.

### How the spec is regenerated

`OpenApiContractIT` fetches `/v3/api-docs` from the integration-test context (which runs against its own MongoDB container) and writes `contracts/openapi.json`, keys sorted and without the test's server URL. It runs on every `./mvnw verify` and behind `./dev.sh generate`; `./mvnw package` does **not** regenerate the spec.

Semantic ids appear in the spec as the JSON they are written as (a string, under the property's real name) because `config/EssentialsWebConfig` registers `SingleValueTypeModelConverter`. Without that bean springdoc describes each id as an object and every generated client is wrong, silently.

After changing an endpoint or a DTO, run `./mvnw verify` (or only that test: `./mvnw -pl backend test-compile failsafe:integration-test failsafe:verify -Dit.test=OpenApiContractIT`) and **commit the regenerated spec in the same change**: an endpoint change whose spec is not regenerated has only half landed.

### How the frontend is built into the JAR

The committed spec is the **input** of the embedded frontend build. On `package`, at prepare-package:

1. `frontend-maven-plugin` installs Node, runs `npm ci`, `npx orval` (reads `contracts/openapi.json`), and `npm run build`.
2. `maven-resources-plugin` copies `frontend/dist/` into the JAR's `static/`, where `SpaWebFilter` serves deep links from `index.html`.

`-Pskip-frontend` skips both, for backend-only work.

### After changing a `@RestController` or DTO

1. Run `./dev.sh generate`: regenerates `contracts/openapi.json` and the TypeScript client.
2. The frontend picks up the new types via HMR; restart the backend (`./dev.sh backend`) to serve the change.
3. Commit `contracts/openapi.json` with the change.

## Frontend mutation & invalidation patterns

### Custom fetch: handle empty response bodies

`frontend/src/shared/api/custom-fetch.ts` reads the body with `response.text()` and parses it only when there is one. Keep it that way: `response.json()` throws `SyntaxError` on the empty body of a void endpoint (200/204), silently breaking every void mutation.

### Pass `onSuccess` at the mutate call site

Keep mutation hooks bare and pass callbacks where `mutate` is called — the core TanStack Query API:

```tsx
const { mutate } = useMyMutation();
mutate(variables, { onSuccess: () => invalidate() });
```

Do **not** route `onSuccess` through the Orval-generated hook options (`useXxx({ mutation: { onSuccess } })`); the option spreading in the generated `getMutationOptions` functions is not a reliable path for callbacks.

## Key conventions

- WebMvc (servlet), not WebFlux. `config/EssentialsWebConfig` imports `EssentialsWebMvcConfigurer`: that `@Import` is what makes Essentials semantic types bind as path variables (S4).
- `SpaWebFilter` forwards non-API paths to `index.html`; it skips `/api/`, `/v3/`, `/swagger-ui`, `/actuator` and any path with a file extension. Do not add CORS configuration or an API base URL: the SPA and the API share an origin.
- `config/SecurityConfig` permits everything. It is a starting point, not a posture: replace it before the application leaves a laptop.
- Package root, Maven coordinates, and stack version pins are listed under **Project pins** at the top of this file — that block is the source of truth, do not duplicate it elsewhere.

## Slicing & anti-god-class rules

Package by **vertical slice**, never by technical layer. The short form of the rules lives in
`.claude/rules/essentials-slices.md`, which loads automatically every session: the directory
vocabulary, the four slice kinds, and the boundary rule.

The full law is the `essentials` plugin's `rules/slice-design.md` — R1–R5, sanctioned sharing, red
flags, and the anti-rationalisation table. Reach it by running `/essentials:add-slice` or
`/essentials:slice-check`, or by asking about Essentials slices (the `essentials-docs` skill routes
there). It is read live from the plugin, so it stays current without this project being updated.

- **Frontend mirrors this:** one component per use-case (no multi-tab god-component), one API module
  per slice, thin pages that compose components.
- **Wiring is part of done:** register each Decider/component (`@Bean`/DI) so the slice's integration
  test can bootstrap — un-wired components silently break `@SpringBootTest`.

Add slices with `/essentials:add-slice` (command / view / automation / translation) rather than by
hand — it emits the source files, the `slice.yaml` manifest, the per-slice `CLAUDE.md`, the test, and
the wiring. Audit with `/essentials:slice-check`.

## When to write PBT

Property-based tests are powerful but expensive — writing one for *every* requirement (including thin
CRUD) wastes hours. Decide per requirement, using the rule below.

- **Warranted (write a property):** a domain invariant on a consistency boundary (e.g. the
  `PlaceOrderDecider` "quantity > 0" rule); a non-trivial input space (parser/validator/calculation/
  serializer/state-machine); financial, security, or temporal logic; round-trip / event rehydration.
- **Not warranted (skip):** thin CRUD, pass-through views /
  projections of already-validated data, pure presentation, trivial getters, BFF orchestration
  (use contract tests).
- **Iterations:** right-size to the input space. With jqwik, a small finite space (an enum, a small
  sealed set, a tiny int range) runs **exhaustively** and ignores `@Property(tries=N)` — a low run
  count there is complete; don't raise `tries`. Use 500+ for critical paths over *wide* domains, and
  make stateful/commutativity properties cover the **full** command set.

## More

For Essentials-specific decisions (event sourcing yes/no, module choice, slice architecture, anti-patterns), invoke the `essentials-docs` skill.
=== FILE README.md (644) ===
# Golden

A full-stack application with a React/TypeScript frontend and a Java/Spring Boot backend. One `mvn package` produces a fat JAR that serves both the API and the SPA.

Built on the [Trustworks Essentials](https://github.com/trustworksdk/essentials-project) framework.

## Architecture

```
LOCAL DEVELOPMENT
-----------------
Browser :5173  -->  Vite Dev Server (HMR)  -->  /api/*  -->  Spring Boot :8080

PRODUCTION
----------
Browser  -->  Spring Boot :8080  -->  /api/*   (API)
                                 -->  /*       (SPA static files)
                                 -->  /swagger-ui.html (API docs)
```

The backend is organised by **vertical slice** within bounded contexts, not by technical layer. Each bounded context gets its own package; `/essentials:add-slice` creates them.

## Project Structure

```
golden/
├── pom.xml                        # Reactor parent: versions and BOM imports
├── dev.sh                         # Development convenience script
├── contracts/
│   └── openapi.json               # API contract, generated by `mvn verify`, committed
│
├── backend/                       # Spring Boot application
│   ├── pom.xml
│   ├── compose.yml                # Development MongoDB
│   └── src/main/java/com/example/golden/
│       ├── Application.java
│       ├── config/                # Application-wide Spring wiring
│       └── <bc>/                  # One package per bounded context (add as you go)
│
└── frontend/                      # React/TypeScript SPA
    ├── orval.config.ts            # OpenAPI → TypeScript codegen
    └── src/
        ├── app/                   # App shell, routing
        ├── shared/api/            # custom-fetch.ts + the generated client (not committed)
        └── domains/               # Per-context UI (add as you go)
```

## Quick Start

### Prerequisites

- Java at the `java.version` in the root `pom.xml`, or newer
- Maven, or the Maven wrapper (`./mvnw`) when the project has one
- Docker, for the integration tests and the development database
- Node.js and npm for frontend development (the Maven build installs its own Node)

### Run

```bash
./dev.sh              # backend + frontend together
./dev.sh backend      # backend only on :8080
./dev.sh frontend     # vite dev server on :5173
./dev.sh generate     # regenerate the TypeScript client from the running backend's spec
```

### Build

```bash
(cd frontend && npm install)   # first time: creates package-lock.json (commit it)
./mvnw package                 # the JAR, with the SPA inside
./mvnw package -Pskip-frontend # backend only, no Node/npm
```

### Test

```bash
./mvnw verify -Pskip-frontend  # unit + integration tests (Docker); regenerates contracts/openapi.json
(cd frontend && npm test -- --run)
```

For a fast inner loop, see `CLAUDE.md` → "Inner-loop integration tests".

## Working with Claude Code

`CLAUDE.md` in this repo tells Claude Code to use the **`essentials` plugin's `essentials-docs` skill** for any framework questions. The skill auto-loads on Essentials-related queries and on code signals (imports, type names) and proactively consults the plugin's design guide before editing code.

If the plugin isn't installed yet:

```
/plugin marketplace add trustworksdk/essentials-project
/plugin install essentials@essentials-marketplace
```

## Configuration

| Setting | Value |
|---|---|
| Project | `Golden` |
| groupId | `com.example` |
| artifactId | `golden` |
| Package root | `com.example.golden` |

